<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rutger Jan Schimmelpenninck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1761">1761</a>–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rutger Jan Schimmelpenninck was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> lawyer and statesman who served as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. In 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> made him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-pensionary">Grand Pensionary</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> with wide powers, and he introduced a national tax system and education reforms. In 1806 Napoleon replaced the republic with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-holland">Kingdom of Holland</a> under his brother Louis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rutger-jan-schimmelpenninck.jpg" alt="Rutger Jan Schimmelpenninck" width="320" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Howard Hodges · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portret_van_Rutger_Jan_Schimmelpenninck_(1761-1825).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Schimmelpenninck was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deventer">Deventer</a>, Overijssel, on 31 October 1761, into an illegitimate branch of the noble family Schimmelpenninck van der Oye. His father, Gerrit Schimmelpenninck, was a wine trader whose Mennonite faith barred him from rights in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. He studied law at Leiden University from 1781 and took his doctorate in 1784 with a thesis defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>'s doctrine of popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, limited to the wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>, and praising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">Constitution of the United States</a>.</p><p>One of the first Patriots at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a>, he helped write the Patriot manifesto known as the Leiden Draft in 1785. He then practised as a lawyer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and married Catharina Nahuys, of a wealthy family. After the Prussian intervention of 1787 restored the stadtholder William V, he and fellow Patriots such as Alexander Gogel founded the society Doctrina et Amicitia. In 1792 he joined the Holland Land Company, which invested in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the Batavian Revolution of 19 January 1795 he sat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>'s temporary government, and in 1796 he was elected to the 1st <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>, where he sat with the Moderates between the conservative Federalists and the radical Unitarians. He presided over the assembly twice. On 14 June 1798 he became ambassador in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he was taken with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> after the coup of 1799, and he took part in the talks before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens">Treaty of Amiens</a>. He served briefly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from 8 December 1802 and was back in Paris by 15 September 1803.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-pensionary">Grand Pensionary</a> from 29 April 1805, ruling under a constitution he had written at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s request, he was in practice dependent on Napoleon. Gogel, his secretary of state for finance, brought in excises on salt, soap, peat, grain and meat, a land tax and a cadastre, and Hendrik van Stralen introduced subsidised public education. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-bonaparte">Louis Bonaparte</a> replaced him on 4 June 1806.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> made him a count of the Empire on 10 April 1811, and from 30 December 1811 he sat in the French Sénat conservateur, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> having been annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. After 1815 he took a seat in the First Chamber of the States General of the United Kingdom of the Netherlands, until an eye disease forced him to retire in 1820. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> on 15 February 1825. The historian Niek Sas called him the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> liberal politician.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rutger Jan Schimmelpenninck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1805 <b>Rutger Jan Schimmelpenninck</b> became Grand Pensionary with wide powers.</blockquote><a href="/state/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deventer">Deventer</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.25,6.17&amp;year=1802" aria-label="Deventer on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4689 360 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rutger Jan Schimmelpenninck"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5139 630h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5139 630h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.25,6.17&amp;year=1802">What was here in 1802? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
